// Two provide methods the combined path carries, one per method-body `let`
// shape the issue #2898 port added.
//
// The first binds an untyped `[]` and returns it: the binding takes the
// method's declared return type, which is `_emit_method_body`'s let arm. An
// annotated `let pinned: List[Str] = []` takes its pin with no inference, and
// the `.length()` read is a `builtin` node, which is what makes the frontend
// stamp the document ir_version 3 (a document of components alone is v1). The
// reference's let arm reads the literal's own `expected` first and only then
// the method's flow hints, so both shapes have to be spelled to exercise both
// arms of that decision.
//
// The second binds a user variant. A provide-method binding of a variant holds
// the variant's interface type, not the concrete case struct (item 280, the
// method-body twin): a later `match` type-switches on it, and Go rejects a
// type switch on a concrete struct. The `match` is what makes the pin
// observable rather than cosmetic: with `var o Outcome` the type switch
// compiles and each arm returns its value; a bare `o := OutcomeMissing{}`
// would make the switch a Go compile error. The declared `type Outcome` is
// also what routes this document to the combined path, so the type, the
// service interfaces and both components ride one package.
type Outcome = Found(Int) | Missing

service Dup { fn dup(xs: List[Str]) -> List[Str] }
component D provides dup: Dup {
  provide dup {
    fn dup(xs) {
      var out = []
      let pinned: List[Str] = []
      let n = out.length()
      return out
    }
  }
}

service Pick { fn pick(n: Int) -> Int }
component P provides pick: Pick {
  provide pick {
    fn pick(n) {
      let o = Missing
      return match o { Missing => 1, Found(v) => v }
    }
  }
}
